Subject: Rebuild the views of a forced task's activity at its new density without a relaunch

DSA gives a task the density of the host monitor its window is on. A task the user set to forced
system scaling takes a new density without a relaunch (0009): the activity, its process, its view
models and its players survive. Its resources then answer at the new density, but its views keep
every pixel size they read at the old one, so an app bar keeps its old height while a list grows
with the items it inflates afresh, and items it reuses come back at the old size.

The window manager now tells the app's process, with each configuration change of such an
activity (ActivityConfigurationChangeItem.mDsaRescaleViews), that its views are rescaled: for an
activity of a marked task whose manifest does not handle density. When the density of the
activity's resources changes, ActivityThread converts the views of its windows (its own, its
dialogs and its popups) to the new density in place, before the activity hears of the change
(android.view.DsaDensityRescale). Each view and each set of layout parameters records the density
its sizes are in. A view that is not in a window then, such as a list item a RecyclerView keeps
for reuse, is converted when it is added to a parent or a window again, or when the app first
reads or sets one of its sizes through the framework, so that a size the app sets at the new
density is not converted twice.

After converting a window's views, the app lays out again at the window's new bounds: the window
asks the window manager for its frame again, the window insets go through the tree again (so an
inset listener works out its padding again, and a CoordinatorLayout hands the insets to its
behaviours again), and every view in the tree measures and lays out again, so a parent that
places its children from what it keeps itself (a CoordinatorLayout with an app bar) places them
again from the converted sizes.

Converted: layout parameters (width, height, margins), padding, minimum size, elevation,
translation, scroll position, the drawables' density (bitmap, nine-patch, vector, animated vector,
shape, layer, state list and inset drawables, in place, so an animation keeps running), a text
view's text size, line spacing, shadow, size limits, auto-size range and compound drawables, and
an image view's drawable and size limits.

A view of the app's own class (or of a library the app ships) may also keep in its own fields
sizes its constructor read from its XML attributes, and lay out with them ever after: a sliding
panel reads its collapsed height (FLO's player panel, 112 dp) into a field once, so after a move
from 262 to 164 dpi it stayed 183 px instead of 115: the player bar stood 68 px above the tab bar
it sits on, and after a move the other way it slid 68 px behind it. The first time such a view is
converted, the element it was inflated from is read again from its layout (the view's source
layout, which every view records), the dimensions written on it and in its style are worked out
at the old density, and each int or float field of the view's own classes that holds one of them
is converted with it, the same fields every time after that. 0 and one pixel either way are never
matched. This runs only when a view is converted, so a process that is never given a forced
density change does nothing more and keeps nothing more; AssetManager.getStyleAttributes becomes
a hidden public method for it.

Not converted: sizes the app works out itself (from the display metrics, from resources it reads
in code, or in objects other than the view), sizes that come only from a view's default style,
sizes in text spans, an image matrix, and drawables an app made without resources.

Nothing changes in a process until the first such change reaches it, and nothing changes for an
activity that handles density itself or for any task that is not marked.

Applies to frameworks/base at 94b4c163b7dfe5ce3607f7bb8456f9573f7de57d (Android 17), after 0009.
